      "primary": {
        "canonical_id": "doi:10.24963/ijcai.2026/655",
        "claim_evidence_alignment": "The central claim that CPA reduces Subject-Dominant Memory Interference is supported by consistent R-Spec. gains over ROME across four model backbones (e.g., 45.7% to 72.2% on GPT2-XL, 70.7% to 92.2% on Qwen2.5 7B) and by lower DP and higher EOS on EVOKE. The mechanistic claim of restored relational mediation is supported by causal tracing RIE comparisons, and the plug-in claim is supported by R-Spec. improvements when CPA is transplanted to MEMIT and AlphaEdit.",
        "context_id": "d5aba638083f7fcadacd84049347c421a546017878e3272dbe8e285e4072afac",
        "decision": "pass",
        "evaluation_assessment": "Evaluation is substantial and appropriate for the contribution. The paper uses two complementary benchmarks, COUNTERFACT RS and EVOKE, across four LLM backbones with 2,000 edits and five repeated evaluations, reporting means and standard deviations. It includes ablations of the two phases, mechanistic causal tracing validation, plug-and-play generalization to MEMIT and AlphaEdit, sensitivity analysis of the anchor layer, computational overhead analysis, and a zsRE evaluation that reveals a limitation. The zsRE results show lower efficacy and generalization, which the authors explicitly report and interpret rather than hide.",
        "evidence_ids": [
          "Ed5aba638083f7fca-B0038",
          "Ed5aba638083f7fca-B0041",
          "Ed5aba638083f7fca-B0042",
